# Whether a number t of T is a member of an interval of the round-up interval: -n <= t <= u and t
# finite, written on the lanes as stored (a comparison with a NaN is false, so the empty set
# contains nothing); the default interval_contains_1 on the meaning. Exact, in every rounding mode
# (docs/developer/design/upward.md, "Predicates and comparisons"). Requires the rounding mode upward
# set by kairn::upward::Scope and the options of docs/concepts/upward.md (GCC and Clang
# -frounding-math, MSVC /fp:strict).
kernel:        upward_contains_1
rounding:      upward
input:         pair stored x = xu xn
input:         t
output:        mask m
requires:      upward xu xn
ensures:       certain m member t x x=xu,xn exact
min_precision: 1
source:        the lanes compared with the point and its negation, and a test that the point is finite (the listing of the round-up interval)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        proved
domain:        extended
ieee_only:     yes
ieee_status:   proved
ieee_theorem:  proofs/theories/IeeeUpwardPredicates.v upward_contains_1_correct
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   the result is a mask, which has no non-finite value, and a comparison with NaN is false; certain states what comes out for every canonical operand

a = le(t, xu)
nt = neg(t)
b = le(nt, xn)
ta = abs(t)
ft = lt(ta, const inf)
ab = and(a, b)
m = and(ab, ft)
